<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vindolanda</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Chesterholm</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vindolanda was a Roman auxiliary fort in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, just south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadrians-wall">Hadrian's Wall</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northumberland">Northumberland</a>. Built around 85 CE, before the wall, it was garrisoned by auxiliary units of Tungrians and Batavians. Excavations directed by Robin Birley from 1973 recovered the Vindolanda tablets, thin wooden writing tablets of around 100 CE that preserve letters, lists and accounts and give a rare picture of daily life on the frontier.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vindolanda.jpg" alt="Vindolanda" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mike Bishop · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kastell_und_Vicus_von_Vindolanda,_Luftbild,_2010.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Vindolanda</b> tablets, letters on wood from a fort near Hadrian's Wall, reveal the routine of this administration around 100.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-britain">Roman Britain · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Tungrian cohorts served across the empire, most famously on Hadrian's Wall and at <b>Vindolanda</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/tungri">Tungri</a></li></ul></section>
